The readings are considered stable when the variability among consecutive measurements falls within the USGS stabilization criteria for each parameter and after the sensors have had time to equilibrate with the water.

* **Equilibration time** – For surface‑water work you should wait at least 60 seconds (or follow the manufacturer’s guidance) before judging the values【3†"Surface water: Allow at least 60 seconds (or follow the manufacturer’s guidelines) for sensors to equilibrate with sample water."】. The same 60‑second minimum is recommended for each new measurement location【4†"Wait a minimum of 60 seconds for the sensors and sonde body to reach thermal equilibrium with the water at each new measurement location."】.

* **Stabilization criteria** – Once the sensors are equilibrated, keep recording until the variability between successive readings is within the limits shown in the USGS table:

  * Temperature ± 0.2 °C【5†"Temperature  ± 0.2 °C"}  
  * Dissolved oxygen ± 0.2 mg/L【5†"DO  ± 0.2 mg/L"}  
  * pH ± 0.1 pH units【5†"pH  ± 0.1 pH units"}  
  * Specific electrical conductance ± 5 µS/cm for SC ≤ 100 µS/cm, or ± 3 % for SC > 100 µS/cm【5†"Specific electrical conductance (SC)  ± 5 μS/cm for SC ≤100 μS/cm, or ± 3% for SC >100 μS/cm"}  
  * Turbidity ± 0.5 TU or 5 % of the measured value (whichever is greater) for turbidity ≤ 100 TU; or 10 % of the measured value for turbidity > 100 TU【5†"Turbidity  ± 0.5 TU or 5% of the measured value, whichever is greater, for turbidity ≤ 100 TU; or 10% of the measured value for turbidity >100 TU"}.

When the readings meet those variability limits after the initial 60‑second equilibration period, you can record the median of the final three (or more) stable measurements as the field value.
